E7 gate — the Linux task's stack high-water (Q2). 2026-11-01.
image:    sls_operating_system_e3_gate.iso — make x86-iso-e3
          E3_INIT_FEATURES=e3_envs,e7_gate E7_SLOW_BOOT=0 E7_TOOTH= (empty:
          the control profile; the make knob is empty-or-1 and refuses 0)
machine:  qemu-system-x86_64 -m 1G -smp 2 -accel tcg,thread=multi, 10 GiB NVMe
invocation: E7G_DRILL=0 E7G_BUILD=0 \
            E7G_ISO=sls_operating_system_e3_gate.iso \
            tests/e7_gate_check.sh --live
method:   the gate profile fills [stack base, entry %rsp) with 0xA5 at create
          and scans the leading untouched run at the stop (boot 1) and at
          teardown (boot 2, whose captured pages carried the pattern through
          the checkpoint). alloc is the whole region; poison-span is the bytes
          below the entry %rsp; headroom is the untouched prefix;
          deepest = poison-span - headroom; lowest = base + headroom.

=== boot 1, at the stop (the work half) ===
[E7-GATE] stack hwm (stop): alloc=65536 poison-span=60912 headroom=58264 deepest=2648 lowest=0x4000000a9398

=== boot 2, at the resumed tenant's teardown (the full run) ===
[E7-GATE] stack hwm (exit): alloc=65536 poison-span=60912 headroom=57424 deepest=3488 lowest=0x4000000a9050

The deepest point of the two boots is 3,488 bytes below the entry %rsp at
lowest=0x4000000a9050: 8,112 bytes used from the region's top (4,096 of them
the BIB page, plus the initial image), 57,424 bytes untouched — 87.6% of the
64 KiB budget. The stop alone saw 2,648 bytes; the resumed tail went 840 bytes
deeper, which is why the full-run number is the teardown one.

The same run's gate clauses all pass (B1–B6; D1/D2 are not applicable because
the plain control image has no drill): "e7_gate_check: the gate run passes —
artifacts in /tmp/e7-gate-256108". The drill-image run from the same tree
(built by the harness before the exit scan existed) reported the same stop
number: headroom=58264 deepest=2648 (artifacts /tmp/e7-gate-245023).

Reproduce: build the control image as above, then the invocation line; the two
lines above are in boot1.log and boot2.log of the artifact directory.
